Three statements.
\begin{enumerate}
\item \emph{(The identification; imported.)} The single-spinor sector (single-twistor sector) of Fact \claimref{neutrino.imported-derived-content-singlets}, the states of one conformal spinor $Z \in \CS$, carries two colour singlets, at grades zero and three, and the observed comparison column ($\bar{3} \oplus \bar{3} \oplus 1 \oplus 1$) always contained two: the right-handed neutrino was in the comparison from the start. In the Standard-Model paper's listing, $\nu_{R}$ (as $\nu^{c}$) is the grade-zero state of the single-spinor sector, the \emph{empty conformal spinor} (the empty twistor) in this paper's usage (the Standard-Model paper uses the same phrases, ``empty conformal spinor'' and ``full conformal spinor'', formerly ``empty twistor'' and ``full twistor'', for the doublet components $(1,0)$ and $(1,3)$ of its one-slot listing, the no-pair doublet $(2,1)_{1/2}$ and the lepton doublet: different objects from the grade-zero and grade-three singlets named here), the pair system with no rung occupied, and its hypercharge is $Y = -0/3 = 0$ by the weight rule; the grade-three state, the filled conformal spinor (filled twistor), has $Y = -1$ and is $e^{c}$ after conjugation. Where the Standard Model treats $\nu_{R}$ as optional, this content carries it necessarily.
\item \emph{(Anomalies could not have fixed it; proved.)} For the content $Q = (2,3)$, $L = (2,1)$, $u^{c}, d^{c} = (1,\bar{3})$, $e^{c} = (1,1)$ and a fourth singlet $N$ of hypercharge $Y_{N}$, all in one handedness, with $Y_{Q} \neq 0$, the solutions of the four anomaly conditions ($[SU(3)]^{2}U(1)$, $[SU(2)]^{2}U(1)$, gravitational, cubic) are \emph{exactly} the two-dimensional span $\{sY + t(B - L)\}$ with $Y_{Q} = \tfrac{s}{6} + \tfrac{t}{3} \neq 0$, up to the relabelling $u^{c} \leftrightarrow d^{c}$, where $Y$ is the derived spectrum with $Y_{N} = 0$ and $B - L$ takes the values $\tfrac13, -\tfrac13, -\tfrac13, -1, 1, 1$ on $Q, u^{c}, d^{c}, L, e^{c}, N$; up to normalisation this is the family $Y + t(B - L)$, with $Y_{N} = t$, together with the ray of $B - L$ itself, on which $Y_{N}/Y_{Q} = 3$. So anomaly cancellation leaves $Y_{N}$ free, and the zero of (1) is a computation of the content derivation, not a consistency condition. With $Y_{N} = 0$ imposed the family collapses to the derived spectrum (Fact \claimref{neutrino.imported-derived-content-singlets}), which is why the Standard-Model paper's uniqueness theorem carries that hypothesis. (Witten's global $SU(2)$ anomaly imposes nothing here: the doublet count is even and a singlet does not enter it.)
\item \emph{(Anomaly-invisible, on the lattice; proved.)} At $Y_{N} = 0$ the fourth singlet contributes to none of the four sums, so deleting it from the spectrum changes none of them, and the $\mathbb{Z}_{6}$ lattice admits its entry. The orientation dependency of the identification is discharged by the flag covariance of Fact \claimref{neutrino.imported-ceiling-under-hypothesis-u-arrow}.
\end{enumerate}

Three statements and one premise.
\begin{enumerate}
\item \emph{(The sixteenth refutation, by magnitude; proved.)} The cosmological term of the deformed bracket (Fact \claimref{mass.exact-join}) cannot supply the neutrino mass. For a composite of extent $l$ and constituent momentum scale $p$, $|[\omega^{1}\omega^{2}]| \sim l^{2}p$ and $|\br{\pi^{1}\pi^{2}}| \sim p$, so the cosmological term lies below the flat term by the factor $\Lambda l^{2}/6$, which reaches one only at $l = \sqrt{6/\Lambda} = \sqrt{2}\,\LH$ with $\LH = \sqrt{3/\Lambda}$ the Hubble length: the $\Lambda$ term never dominates for anything smaller than the horizon. Read as a mass on its own (a composite with $\br{\pi^{1}\pi^{2}} = 0$ and scalar Gram matrix, $m = \sqrt{2}\,\tfrac{\Lambda}{6}\,|[\omega^{1}\omega^{2}]|$), a $\Lambda$-term neutrino of $0.05$ eV with constituents at its own momentum scale has extent $l = 2^{1/4}\LH \approx 1.19\,\LH$, and with weak-scale constituents an extent of order $10^{20}$ m. The Casimir branch is no better: $m^{2} = \tfrac{\Lambda}{3}C$ at $0.05$ eV needs $C$ of order $10^{63}$. The scope is exact: this refutes the cosmological term as a neutrino mass mechanism; it is not a statement about a compositeness floor, which Fact \claimref{mass.exact-join} says does not exist.
\item \emph{(The neutral bilinear; elementary, proved.)} In the field-theory reading of the derived content, the hypercharge of a fermion bilinear is the sum of the two fields' hypercharges. Among the derived fermions, the bilinear $N N$ of the total singlet with itself is the unique neutral one, hence the unique fermion mass term that gauge invariance permits without a condensate; every other bilinear, the Dirac neutrino term $L N$ included, carries a nonzero hypercharge that only the condensate can absorb, and forms at the electroweak condensate scale $v \approx 246$ GeV \cite{PDG2024}. This is the textbook statement and uses nothing from the framework's bracket, which says nothing about that bilinear (Fact \claimref{neutrino.imported-mass-bracket-quantum-numbers}).
\end{enumerate}
\begin{premise}[M: the framework realises the Majorana term, at a scale $M \gg v$]
The neutral bilinear of (2) is realised by the framework, is nonzero, and its scale $M$ lies far above $v$. What (2) proves is that nothing forbids the term; gauge invariance says nothing about whether it forms or about its magnitude ($M = 0$, Dirac neutrinos; $M \sim v$; $M \sim$ eV are all consistent with it), and the framework's own mass bracket, computed only on slot states, undefined for excited composites, and of unsettled relation to the order parameter (Fact \claimref{neutrino.imported-mass-bracket-quantum-numbers}), has not been identified with it, nor shown to differ from it. This paper takes (M) as a premise, values $M$ from the observed masses in Remark \claimref{note.neutrino.valuation-m-both-ends-hypothesis-u}, and names its derivation as a successor problem.
\end{premise}
\begin{enumerate}
\setcounter{enumi}{2}
\item \emph{(Block inversion; proved.)} Under (M), for the mass matrix $\begin{psmallmatrix} 0 & \mD \\ \mD^{\mathsf{T}} & M_{R}\end{psmallmatrix}$ with $M_{R}$ invertible and $\|\mD\| \ll \|M_{R}\|$, the light block is $-\mD M_{R}^{-1}\mD^{\mathsf{T}}$ at leading order, symmetric (Majorana); the sign is a Majorana phase and is dropped hereafter. In the one-generation case, with $m_{D}$ and $M_{R}$ scalars,
\begin{equation}
m_{\nu} \;=\; \frac{m_{D}^{2}}{M_{R}} ,
\end{equation}
with the exact small eigenvalue differing from it by less than $m_{D}^{4}/M_{R}^{3}$.
\end{enumerate}

Let $Y_{D}$ be the Dirac neutrino Yukawa matrix and $\mD = vY_{D}$ the Dirac mass matrix. The Dirac neutrino brackets are face-valued like every Yukawa (Fact \claimref{neutrino.imported-indifference-faces-democratic-alignment-rank}, extended here to the bracket of $L$ with $N$, this paper's step, and one that reaches past Fact \claimref{neutrino.imported-mass-bracket-quantum-numbers}'s caveat, $L$ being the full conformal spinor (the full twistor) with all three pair modes occupied) and inherit the vacuum's democratic alignment, so $\mD = d\,\J$ at leading order, with the single Dirac eigenvalue $m_{D} := 3d$ along $u'$. The Majorana matrix $M_{R}$ involves no condensate, so the indifference instrument applied to it (a structural-level application, at the level Fact \claimref{neutrino.imported-indifference-faces-democratic-alignment-rank} holds the instrument) forbids any face anisotropy at leading order: $M_{R}$ is $S_{3}$-invariant, hence $M_{R} = \alpha\mathbf{1} + \beta\J$ by Lemma \claimref{neutrino.invariant-symmetric-matrices}, with $u'$ an eigenvector of eigenvalue $M_{3} = \alpha + 3\beta$; and if the Gram-square reading, which the families paper states for the condensate's source, is extended to the condensate-free $M_{R}$ (this paper's extension of that reading beyond the object it was stated for, at the same structural level), then $M_{R} \propto \J$ at leading order, so $\alpha = 0$ there and $\alpha$ is of the order of the breaking. Only $M_{3} \neq 0$ is assumed in (0).
\begin{enumerate}
\setcounter{enumi}{-1}
\item \emph{(The leading-order scalar seesaw on the democratic block; proved.)} The Dirac coupling $\mD$ annihilates $u'^{\perp}$ on the right, so the neutrino system splits into the democratic block, spanned by $\nu_{u'}$ and $N_{u'}$ with mass matrix $\begin{psmallmatrix} 0 & m_{D} \\ m_{D} & M_{3}\end{psmallmatrix}$, and the two states $N_{\perp}$ of $u'^{\perp}$, of Majorana mass $\alpha$ and no Dirac coupling. The light matrix is therefore exactly rank one along $u'$, with the single light mass
\[
m_{3} \;=\; \frac{m_{D}^{2}}{M_{3}} \qquad(\text{leading order in } m_{D}/M_{3}),
\]
whatever $\alpha$ is, $\alpha = 0$ included, in which case $N_{\perp}$ are two exactly massless, exactly decoupled sterile states at leading order.
\item \emph{(Ordering.)} Normal. Inverted ordering needs its two heaviest states degenerate to two percent ($m_{1}/m_{2} \ge 0.98$ at the measured splittings); a rank-one light matrix has one nonzero eigenvalue, and corrections small compared with it give one heavy state and two light, not a degenerate pair. Excluded by rank plus the smallness of the breaking, not by fit.
\item \emph{(The sum.)} At leading order $m_{1} = m_{2} = 0$. For any spectrum with $m_{1} \ge 0$ and normal ordering the sum obeys the kinematic bound $\mnu \ge \sqrt{\Delta m^{2}_{21}} + \sqrt{\Delta m^{2}_{31}}$, which at the splittings $\Delta m^{2}_{21} \in (7.3, 7.7) \times 10^{-5}$ eV$^{2}$ and $\Delta m^{2}_{31} \in (2.45, 2.55) \times 10^{-3}$ eV$^{2}$ \cite{PDG2024, NuFIT2024} is the floor $0.0580$-$0.0593$ eV; the residue is $\mnu - \text{floor} \approx m_{1}$ at small $m_{1}$, and the script certifies the sum inside $(0.0591, 0.0604)$ eV at $m_{1} = 1$ meV, above $0.0616$ eV at $3$ meV, and above $0.0736$ eV at $10$ meV.
\end{enumerate}
\begin{premise}[H: the second stage is hierarchical]
The breaking $S_{3} \to S_{2} \to 1$ lifts $m_{1}$ by a factor small compared with the factor that lifts $m_{2}$: $m_{1}/m_{2} \lesssim 0.01$, that is $m_{1} \lesssim 0.1$ meV. The charged sectors supply the analogy at $m_{e}/m_{\mu} \approx 0.005$ and $m_{u}/m_{c} \approx 0.002$ \cite{PDG2024}; by Lemma \claimref{neutrino.breaking-relation} the light masses go as the \emph{squared} singular values of the compressed breaking where the charged masses go as the singular values themselves, so a shared direction would give $m_{1}/m_{2} \approx (m_{e}/m_{\mu})^{2}$, far below what (H) needs, and if the directions are not shared the analogy is only that hierarchies are the rule. Under (H) the sum is the floor to within about $0.1$ meV ($0.1007$ meV at $m_{1} = 0.1$ meV, the residue being $m_{1}$ plus the small lift of $m_{2}$ and $m_{3}$), and the double-beta values of (3) hold to within $0.1$ meV; without it the sum is a lower bound, with $m_{1}$ free up to order $m_{2}$, where it would reach roughly $0.07$ eV.
\end{premise}
\begin{enumerate}
\setcounter{enumi}{2}
\item \emph{(The double-beta values.)} $m_{\beta\beta} = |\sum_{i}U_{ei}^{2}m_{i}|$ with $U_{e1} = c_{12}c_{13}$, $U_{e2} = s_{12}c_{13}e^{i\phi/2}$, $U_{e3} = s_{13}e^{-i\delta_{CP}}$ up to the second Majorana phase; under (H) the $m_{1}$ term is below $0.1$ meV. If the breaking is real in the vacuum's basis (the real-breaking assumption of Proposition \claimref{neutrino.leptogenesis-suppressed-alignment-reheating-premise-new}(3)), the phases are $0$ or $\pi$ and $m_{\beta\beta}$ is one of two values: $3.5$-$3.9$ meV at relative phase $0$ or $1.3$-$1.7$ meV at relative phase $\pi$, at $\sin^{2}\theta_{12} \in (0.295, 0.319)$ and $\sin^{2}\theta_{13} \in (0.0216, 0.0227)$ \cite{NuFIT2024}; without that assumption it ranges over the hull, $1.3$-$3.9$ meV. Either way the decay exists, the masses being Majorana under (M), and lies below the reach of the next generation of searches, whose projected sensitivity is a band near $10$-$20$ meV, its width the nuclear matrix element, against a current limit of $28$-$122$ meV \cite{LEGEND2021, KamLANDZen2023}: a conditional absence.
\end{enumerate}

The heavy neutrinos of Proposition \claimref{neutrino.rank-one-seesaw-ordering-sum-double} are the textbook mechanism of leptogenesis, and had that mechanism operated it would have generated the asymmetry in this tower alone, breaking the universality on which the dark-tower count and the arrow's accounting stand (Fact \claimref{neutrino.imported-ceiling-under-hypothesis-u-arrow}). The mechanism is suppressed in three steps, and the danger was double.
\begin{enumerate}
\item \emph{(The alignment result; proved.)} The decay asymmetry of the heavy state $N_{i}$ requires interference \cite{FukugitaYanagida1986, DavidsonNardiNir2008}, $\epsilon_{i} \propto \sum_{j \neq i}\mathrm{Im}\bigl[\bigl((Y_{D}^{\dagger}Y_{D})_{ij}\bigr)^{2}\bigr]\,f(M_{j}/M_{i})$, with $Y_{D}$ written in the heavy-mass eigenbasis. With $Y_{D} = y\J$ (Fact \claimref{neutrino.imported-indifference-faces-democratic-alignment-rank}) and $M_{R} = \alpha\mathbf{1} + \beta\J$ (Lemma \claimref{neutrino.invariant-symmetric-matrices}), every heavy eigenbasis contains $u'$ (for $\beta \neq 0$, which the branch supplies; at $\beta = 0$ every basis is an eigenbasis and the conclusion holds a fortiori), and in any such basis the representation of $Y_{D}^{\dagger}Y_{D} = 3|y|^{2}\J$ has exactly one nonzero entry, on the diagonal at $u'$; every off-diagonal entry vanishes identically, so $\epsilon_{i} = 0$ for every $i$ at leading order, and the pair $N_{\perp}$ has no Dirac coupling at all. What this suppresses is the state $N_{u'}$; the pair is suppressed at leading order only because it does not couple.
\item \emph{(The degenerate pair, and the reheating premise.)} The graver threat was erasure, not production: the rank-one coupling washes exactly one lepton-flavour direction, the democratic one, with strength $K = m_{3}/m_{*} \approx 45$ for the equilibrium mass $m_{*} \in (1.06, 1.15)$ meV \cite{DavidsonNardiNir2008}, and a flavour-democratic descent asymmetry lies entirely in that direction; had the plasma ever reached $T \sim M_{3}$, the framework's own neutrinos would have erased the arrow's imprint. And the pair $N_{\perp}$, exactly degenerate at $\alpha$ at leading order, is the resonant configuration \cite{PilaftsisUnderwood2004}: after the breaking its couplings are $O(\delta)$ but the interference combination that controls its asymmetry, an off-diagonal entry of $Y_{D}^{\dagger}Y_{D}$ normalised by the two diagonal entries it connects, is $O(1)$ in $\delta$, so the pair's asymmetry is not switched off by the smallness of the breaking, and its value depends on the pair's split and widths, which this paper does not compute. What protects both is a premise, stated as such at the scale that does so:
\end{enumerate}
\begin{premise}[R: the descent reheats below the lower heavy scale]
$T_{\mathrm{desc}} \le \alpha = \min(\alpha, M_{3})$, with $\alpha$ on the branch of Remark \claimref{note.neutrino.branch-m-3}. This is a reading of the dark-matter paper's own assumption (Fact \claimref{neutrino.imported-ceiling-under-hypothesis-u-arrow}: $T_{\mathrm{desc}}$ at the ceiling or below it) at "below"; under it no heavy state ever thermalised, the washout of $N_{u'}$ is Boltzmann-suppressed, the pair $N_{\perp}$ was never produced thermally, and the descent's $B - L$ is untouched. The natural justification, that the frozen phase's description dissolves above the rung scale, identifies $M_{3}$ with that scale, which is Hypothesis (U) and the valuation of Remark \claimref{note.neutrino.valuation-m-both-ends-hypothesis-u}, not a theorem; and (R) at $\alpha$ asks more of the descent than that, since $\alpha$ lies nearly two orders below $M_{3}$ on the branch taken. A derivation of $M$ and of $\alpha$ (successor problems (2) and (3)) would decide it.
\end{premise}
\begin{enumerate}
\setcounter{enumi}{2}
\item \emph{(The phases.)} Non-thermal decays of descent-populated heavy states inject asymmetry at order $\delta^{2}\sin\varphi$ from $N_{u'}$, and with an $O(1)$ prefactor times $\sin\varphi$ from the pair by (2), with $\delta$ the breaking of Lemma \claimref{neutrino.breaking-relation} and $\varphi$ a relative phase between the Dirac and Majorana matrices after it. Both matrices are built from one real geometric vacuum and one flavour-independent flag; the orientation is the only CP-violating mechanism the framework itself supplies (Fact \claimref{neutrino.imported-ceiling-under-hypothesis-u-arrow}, uniqueness not claimed there); that it is flavour-independent, one bit of the descent with no face index, as is the relaxing $\theta$-angle that Fact names as unexcluded, is this paper's reading of that object at observation level, and under it neither can generate a \emph{relative} phase between two family matrices; if the breaking is real in the vacuum's basis the relative phases vanish and the injection is zero to all orders in it. Under (R) a consistency consequence follows: the unification scale, on either of the Standard-Model paper's branches (Remark \claimref{note.neutrino.valuation-m-both-ends-hypothesis-u}), lies above $M_{3}$ at either end of Hypothesis (U), so the universe never revisited unification after the descent, and the meeting of the couplings, where there is one, is structural, not thermal history.
\end{enumerate}
The corollary is registered as the framework's fifth stance: the Dirac phase of lepton mixing is trivial, $\delta_{CP} \in \{0, \pi\}$. The current record leaves this allowed and, notably, allowed precisely in the ordering the framework predicts: the joint long-baseline analysis finds CP conservation allowed within the three-sigma interval for \emph{normal} ordering while excluding it at three sigma under \emph{inverted} ordering \cite{T2KNOvA2025}, so the stance's survival and the framework's ordering prediction are now the same experimental question. Should $\delta_{CP}$ prove nontrivial with normal ordering established, the real-breaking assumption fails, the double-beta values of Proposition \claimref{neutrino.rank-one-seesaw-ordering-sum-double}(3) revert to their hull, and subdominance would demand the injected asymmetry, $\delta^{2}\sin\varphi$ from $N_{u'}$ and $O(\sin\varphi)$ from the pair, below the asymmetry a successful leptogenesis needs, of order $10^{-7}$ in the standard treatments \cite{DavidsonNardiNir2008}, a tuning this framework would then have to record. (1) is proved at leading order; (2) is a premise; (3) is conditional on the real-breaking assumption.

Three clauses, at three levels. Gravity is a gravitational field $(\hb, \Omega)$ on flat spacetime with derived metric $g$ (Definition \claimref{obj.synthesis.gtg-flat-spacetime-gauge-fields}); the tractor connection is its $\Cl(2,4)$ gauge field (Theorem \claimref{synthesis.gtg-tractor-gauge-field-reproduces-tractor-connection}).
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(i)] \emph{(Proved.)} On the Einstein region the frozen point is parallel (Fact \claimref{dark-energy.imported-frozen-point-geometry}) and the tractor connection preserves the fibre metric, so $\partial_aQ(I) = 2\ip{\nabla_a I}{I} = 0$: the norm $\Lambda/3$ cannot vary in space or time. A constant $\Lambda$ enters the Einstein equations as the stress-energy $T^{\Lambda}_{ab} = (\Lambda/8\pi G)\,g_{ab}$, that is $T^{\Lambda}(a) = (\Lambda/8\pi G)\,a$ (in the store's convention, where de Sitter space has $\Ric = \Lambda g$ in signature $(+,-,-,-)$, field strength $\mathcal{R}(a) = \Lambda a$, and the Einstein equation reads $\Ric + 8\pi G\,T = (\Lambda + 4\pi G\,g^{ab}T_{ab})\,g$), with energy density $\rho = \Lambda/8\pi G > 0$ for $\Lambda > 0$ and pressure $p = -\rho$ identically: $w = -1$.
\item[(ii)] \emph{(Proved.)} Off the Einstein region write $I_g$ again for the scale tractor of a general field's derived metric $g$ in its own scale, $I_g = (1, 0, J/4) = \nInf + \tfrac{J}{4}\nO$ (the object written $I$ above is its Einstein case, $J/4 = \Lambda/6$), with $J = P_a{}^a$ the trace of the Schouten tensor, the slot that makes $I_g$ parallel exactly when $g$ is Einstein (proved below); by $\ip{(\sigma,\mu,\rho)}{(\sigma',\mu',\rho')} \;=\; g(\mu,\mu') + \sigma\rho' + \rho\sigma'$, $Q(I_g) = J/2 = R/12$, which equals $\Lambda/3$ precisely when $R = 4\Lambda$. On a matter-dominated cosmology $R$ is not constant, so the naive extension of the identification to the epochs at which $w$ is measured does not give a constant.
\item[(iii)] \emph{(Registered, conditional.)} If dark energy is the norm of the frozen point (Theorem \claimref{dark-energy.frozen-point-makes-stelle-west-vector}), and if the frozen point admits a definition on a matter-filled region that keeps it parallel (successor problem (3) of the section ``The engineering conclusion, and scope''), then $w = -1$ exactly, at every epoch, with no evolution and no quintessence. This is a maximally rigid stance in a field of flexible proposals: most dark-energy models add freedom to $\Lambda$CDM, this one removes it. \emph{The confrontation, recorded as of 2026:} the second data release of the Dark Energy Spectroscopic Instrument \cite{DESI2025}, combining baryon-acoustic-oscillation, cosmic-microwave-background and supernova data, reports a preference for an evolving equation of state at roughly three to four standard deviations, with significance depending on the supernova compilation. The preference is contested on three grounds (it concentrates in the derivative of $w$ rather than any mean offset from $-1$; it is prior-sensitive; the driving dataset combinations exhibit internal tensions), so it is not a detection. The framework does not rely on the dispute and states its exposure plainly: a confirmed detection of $w \neq -1$ at any epoch refutes the frozen-point reading of dark energy, with no parameter available to absorb the result. The condition in (iii) is the one way the reading could avoid such a refutation, and the paper names it rather than uses it: a definition of the frozen point off the vacuum region that made the norm track $R/12$ would be refuted already.
\end{enumerate}

Two results move Conjecture \claimref{families.three-generations-faces-maximal-composite}.
\begin{enumerate}
\item \emph{(The missing ingredient identified, at structural level.)} Proposition \claimref{families.three-identical-families-mixing-shared-slots} makes the three faces $S_{3}$-identical, while physical families differ by mass alone; identifying faces with families therefore requires $S_{3}$ to break. The framework's only $S_{3}$-charged vacuum object is the face-valued bracket: the mass bracket is face-valued, one bracket per two-spinor face, so the vacuum of Propositions \claimref{families.bracket-fixed-join} and \claimref{families.source-term-linear-tilt-mass-functional} is a direction in face space, and its alignment spontaneously breaks $S_{3}$, ordering the faces by their overlap with it. The face identification is not an additional postulate awaiting new structure: it is the condensate's alignment, and what that expectation value does and does not determine is exactly what Proposition \claimref{families.alignment-computed-thirteenth-refutation-democracy-one} states.
\item \emph{(The first numerical test, at observation level.)} Single-slot overlap with an aligned condensate is a nearest-neighbour Yukawa texture: direct transitions between the two faces not sharing the condensate's slots are doubly suppressed. The classic consequence of such textures is the Cabibbo relation $\theta_{C} \approx \sqrt{m_{d}/m_{s}} \approx 0.22$, against the observed $0.225$: with $m_{d} = 4.70$, $m_{s} = 93.5$ MeV \cite{PDG2024} the square root is $0.224$, and the conjecture's corollary shape, confronted with its first number, passes at the percent level the quark-mass uncertainties allow. The test inherits the standard assumptions of the texture literature and is flagged at that level; its failure at precision would have refuted the corollary, and did not.
\end{enumerate}
The residue is the alignment's anisotropic part, which depends on the one expectation value Proposition \claimref{families.source-term-linear-tilt-mass-functional} names.

Let $I_{\Lambda} = n_{\infty} + \lambda n_{0}$ with $\lambda = \Lambda/6$ be the frozen point of Lemma \claimref{mass.de-sitter-scale-frozen-point}, and $Z^{1}, Z^{2} \in \CS$ two conformal spinors (twistors).
\begin{enumerate}
\item \emph{(The deformed bracket.)} The pairing of the composite against the frozen point is
\begin{equation}
B_{\Lambda} := \ip{I_{\Lambda}}{Z^{1}\wedge Z^{2}} = \br{\pi^{1}\pi^{2}} + \frac{\Lambda}{6}\,[\omega^{1}\omega^{2}],
\end{equation}
and at $\Lambda = 0$ it is the classical contraction of the composite with the point at infinity $n_{\infty}$, read as a bivector of $\CS$ (Penrose's infinity twistor).
\item \emph{(The mass.)} The grip $P = M(I_{\Lambda})$ has squared length
\begin{equation}
m^{2} = Q(P) = 2\,|B_{\Lambda}|^{2} + Q(I_{\Lambda})\, C = 2\,|B_{\Lambda}|^{2} + \frac{\Lambda}{3}\, C .
\end{equation}
\item \emph{(The flat shadow.)} At $\Lambda = 0$, $m^{2} = 2|\ip{I}{Z^{1}\wedge Z^{2}}|^{2} = 2|\br{\pi^{1}\pi^{2}}|^{2}$: Proposition \claimref{mass.mass-symplectic-area-two-twistor-formula}, read as the squared pairing of the composite against the frozen point.
\item \emph{(The point-supported composite.)} If both spinors lie on the line $L_{x}$ of the point $x$, the composite bivector is $\br{\pi^{1}\pi^{2}}X(x)$ and $B_{\Lambda} = \sigma(x)\br{\pi^{1}\pi^{2}} = \bigl(1 - \tfrac{\Lambda Q(x)}{12}\bigr)\br{\pi^{1}\pi^{2}}$: the flat bracket read in the de Sitter scale.
\item \emph{(The massless locus.)} For $\Lambda > 0$, $m^{2} \ge 0$, and $m^{2} = 0$ if and only if $B_{\Lambda} = 0$ and $h$ is a multiple of the identity; that multiple is zero, so the condition is $B_{\Lambda} = 0$ and $h = 0$ (\claimref{synthesis.many-twistor-join}(v)). The doubled composite $Z^{2} = Z^{1}$ has $B_{\Lambda} = 0$ and $m^{2} = \tfrac{\Lambda}{3}\,(\bar{Z}\cdot Z)^{2}$, so it is massless only when its constituent is null; and there are massless composites that are not doubled, for instance $Z^{1} = e_{1} + e_{4}$, $Z^{2} = \alpha e_{1} + \beta e_{4}$ with $\alpha \neq \beta$.
\end{enumerate}

On the real slice, where $\varepsilon^{A'B'}$ is the conjugate of $\varepsilon^{AB}$ and $\bar\pi$ the conjugate of $\pi$ (the conjugation reverses the complex unit $I_{6}$): a single conformal spinor (twistor) carries null momentum $p_{AA'} = \pi_{A}\bar\pi_{A'}$ and is massless, and a system of two conformal spinors with momentum
\[
p_{AA'} = \pi^{1}_{A}\bar\pi^{1}_{A'} + \pi^{2}_{A}\bar\pi^{2}_{A'}
\]
has
\begin{equation}
m^{2} = p^{2} = 2\,\bigl|\br{\pi^{1}\pi^{2}}\bigr|^{2},
\end{equation}
the squared symplectic area between the two spinor parts. Aligned spinors (vanishing bracket) give a null composite, and mass grows with the misalignment.

The global gauge group of Theorem \claimref{standard-model.gauge-algebra-minimal-massive-composite} is determined exactly. The kernel of the $(U(2) \times U(3))$-action on $\C^{2} \otimes \C^{3}$ is $K = \{(\lambda I_{2}, \lambda^{-1} I_{3})\} \cong U(1)$, and
\begin{equation}
(U(2) \times U(3))/K \;\cong\; S(U(2) \times U(3)) \;\cong\; \bigl(SU(2) \times SU(3) \times U(1)\bigr)/\mathbb{Z}_{6}:
\end{equation}
the framework's group is the smallest of the Standard Model's global forms, the full $\mathbb{Z}_{6}$ quotient, not the naive product and not either intermediate quotient. Three consequences. (i) $S(U(2) \times U(3))$ is exactly the subgroup of $SU(5)$ preserving the splitting $\C^{5} = \C^{2} \oplus \C^{3}$, and none of the three proper covers $(SU(2) \times SU(3) \times U(1))/\Gamma$, $\Gamma \subsetneq \mathbb{Z}_{6}$, acts faithfully through it; so the framework's global form is the one that grand unification's embedding requires, and the trace ratio $3/8$ of the section ``Unification and the desert'' refers to the same normalisation from the other side. (ii) The representations of the group are confined to the $\mathbb{Z}_{6}$ charge lattice: on a representation of the covering product with colour triality $t \in \mathbb{Z}_{3}$, weak parity $\delta \in \mathbb{Z}_{2}$ (the class of the $SU(2)$ content: $1$ on a doublet, $0$ on a singlet or triplet) and abelian weight $q \in \mathbb{Z}$ (the integer such that the $U(1)$ factor $\lambda$ acts by $\lambda^{q}$), descent to the quotient holds if and only if $2t + 3\delta + q \equiv 0 \pmod 6$; writing $Y = q/6$ this is the condition that $t/3 + \delta/2 + Y$ be an integer. The content of Theorem \claimref{standard-model.hypercharge-global-form-pair-counting-consistency} lies on it (each of its representations is built as a representation of $S(U(2) \times U(3))$), and the lattice check is printed there. (iii) A structural stance, registered: the lattice forbids representations off it, so a confirmed discovery of, for example, a fractionally charged colour singlet would refute the identification of the gauge group with the automorphism group of minimal massive compositeness, a stance that current searches test \cite{PDG2024}. The stance inherits the conditionality of Theorem \claimref{standard-model.gauge-algebra-minimal-massive-composite}'s premise and is flagged at that level.

Under $S(U(2) \times U(3))$, with $Y$ the hypercharge just defined:
\begin{enumerate}
\item (Unconditional.) The pair-level generators, the cells of the tensor state space, transform as $(2, 3)$ with abelian weight $q = 1$, so $Y = 1/6$: the quark doublet's representation. A single pair has one unit of hypercharge.
\item (Under Hypothesis (S).) The states with one slot mode occupied and the pair modes in an $SU(3)$-invariant configuration are $\C^{2} \otimes \Lambda^{0}W$, weight $3$ ($Y = +1/2$, total mode number one, odd), and $\C^{2} \otimes \Lambda^{3}W$, weight $-3$ ($Y = -1/2$, total mode number four, even); both transform as $(2, 1)$. Without (S) neither exists in the state space, whose only row-filling states form the quadruplet $(4,1)$ of weight $+3$.
\item (Unconditional as a computation on the Fock space of a single conformal spinor, with the pair-slot factor acting alone; that Fock space is not a subspace of the tensor state space, and under (S) it is the row $j = 0$.) In the sector of a single conformal spinor (the single-twistor sector) $\Lambda^{\bullet}W = \Lambda^{\bullet}\C^{3}$, the grade-$k$ states transform as $\Lambda^{k}\C^{3}$, that is as $1, 3, \bar{3}, 1$ for $k = 0, 1, 2, 3$, with weight $q = -2k$: $Y = 0, -1/3, -2/3, -1$.
\end{enumerate}
In particular, under (S), the fermion content of the composite at the particle handedness (Theorem \claimref{standard-model.hypercharge-global-form-pair-counting-consistency}) is
\begin{equation}
(2,3)_{1/6} \oplus (2,1)_{-1/2},
\end{equation}
quark doublet plus lepton doublet, with colour neutrality of the lepton the statement that a state with its pair modes full is an $SU(3)$ singlet: a lepton is a complete conformal spinor, one slot with the pair system full, while a quark is one slot with one pair.

Under $\su(2) \oplus \su(3) \oplus \uu(1)$, with the abelian charge the pair number, the full Fock space of the minimal massive hierarchy decomposes as
\begin{equation}
\Lambda^{\bullet}(\C^{2} \otimes \C^{3}) = (1,1) \oplus (2,3) \oplus \bigl[(3,\bar{3}) \oplus (1,6)\bigr] \oplus \bigl[(4,1) \oplus (2,8)\bigr] \oplus \bigl[(3,3) \oplus (1,\bar{6})\bigr] \oplus (2,\bar{3}) \oplus (1,1),
\end{equation}
graded by pair number $0$ through $6$; and the sector of a single conformal spinor (the single-twistor sector, massless) decomposes as $\Lambda^{\bullet}\C^{3} = 1 \oplus 3 \oplus \bar{3} \oplus 1$, graded $0$ through $3$.
